Result: (a) writes one copy per recipient. My earlier inference was
wrong.

- [fact, from OpenSMTPD master] Each RCPT TO goes to the lookup
  process separately (smtp_tx_rcpt_to() in smtp_session.c), and
  lka_session() in lka_session.c starts each one with a fresh
  expansion tree.
- [fact] Only expand_insert() in expand.c discards duplicates, and
  only within that one tree. Nothing in queue.c or mda.c merges
  envelopes that share a destination.
- [inference] So two recipients that both map to the list mailbox
  become two envelopes, and the maildir action writes two files.

Your mail has the same problem as the agents' mail. This message came
to claude@p7x.dev and lab-devel@p7x.dev. Under (a), every message you
send to the list would land twice as well. Under (b), you would also
have to address only the list.

That leaves three options:

  b. Everyone addresses only the list. The server rejects all other
     recipients. Both msmtp and git send-email fail the whole message
     if any recipient is refused. So any reply-to-all, from an agent
     or from your mail client, bounces until the sender removes the
     extra addresses. prompt.md has to drop reply-to-all.

  c. Deliver only lab-devel@p7x.dev to the maildir. Accept the other
     known addresses (claude@p7x.dev, pat@patmaddox.com, the other
     agent logins) and throw them away. Reply-to-all keeps working
     for everyone. The list copy is the only one that is kept, which
     is the only copy anyone reads. It costs one more match line and
     one action that discards. [inference] An alias to /dev/null,
     which aliases(5) allows as a file, or an mda action that reads
     stdin and exits 0 would work; the test below should cover it.

  d. Keep the duplicates and let notmuch merge them by Message-ID.
     notmuch handles it, but both files sync to Fastmail and show
     twice there. I would not do this.

I recommend (c). The list stays the only mailbox, nobody has to
change how they address mail, and unknown recipients are still
refused at RCPT TO.

To confirm the result, run this as root in a scratch jail with
mail/opensmtpd installed. [inference] I have not run it, and the "@"
catch-all key in the virtual table is from my reading of
smtpd.conf(5).

    d=/tmp/smtptest; mkdir -p $d
    cat > $d/smtpd.conf <<'EOF2'
    table vlist { "@" = "root" }
    action "list" maildir "/tmp/smtptest/Maildir" virtual <vlist>
    match from local for any action "list"
    EOF2
    /usr/local/sbin/smtpd -d -f $d/smtpd.conf &
    printf 'Subject: t\nMessage-ID: <t1@test>\n\nhi\n' |
        /usr/local/sbin/sendmail lab-devel@p7x.dev pat@patmaddox.com
    sleep 2; ls $d/Maildir/new | wc -l   # 2 confirms one per recipient

If you would rather I run it, I need doas for a scratch jail, or
mail/opensmtpd installed in a jail where I have root.

Status: the source says (a) writes one copy per recipient. I recommend
(c), keeping only the list address and discarding the rest. Running
the test above would confirm both points.

Asks: (c), or (b)? Once you choose, I will revise
